night on c6\n- Gains central space\n- Forces Black to waste time moving the knight again\n- Opens lines for my pieces\n- Follows the principle of "when your opponent develops a piece, consider attacking it if you can gain tempo"\n\nReason: Playing d4-d5 forces Black\'s only developed piece (the knight on c6) to retreat, gaining tempo and central space. This move follows sound opening principles by controlling the center while disrupting Black\'s development. After the knight retreats, I can continue with piece development (like B: f1-c4 or B: c1-g5) with a superior position.\n\nAnswer: P: d4-d5'}
